("premiere",None,"Tosca premiered at the Teatro Costanzi in Rome on 14 January 1900, a day late, because of fears of unrest.",["It premiered at the Teatro Costanzi in Rome on 14 January 1900.","its first performance was delayed for a day for fear of disturbances"]),
("premiere",None,"Police had warnings of an anarchist bomb at the premiere. The conductor, Mugnone, was told to strike up the royal march in an emergency.","Police received warnings of an anarchist bombing of the theatre, and instructed Mugnone","Premiere"),
("premiere",None,"The critics were lukewarm, but the public loved it: the premiere was followed by twenty performances, all to packed houses.",["Much of the critical and press reaction was lukewarm","the premiere was followed by twenty performances, all given to packed houses"],"Premiere"),
("composer",None,"Puccini begged his publisher in 1889 to get Sardou\u2019s permission: \u201cI see in this Tosca the opera I need, with no overblown proportions, no elaborate spectacle.\u201d","I see in this Tosca the opera I need, with no overblown proportions, no elaborate spectacle","Background","Puccini, letter to Giulio Ricordi, 7 May 1889 (via Wikipedia)"),
("artist",None,"The young Enrico Caruso hoped to create Cavaradossi but was passed over for the more experienced Emilio De Marchi.","The young Enrico Caruso had hoped to create the role of Cavaradossi, but was passed over in favour of the more experienced Emilio De Marchi.","Premiere"),
("music","^Act I$|Ah! Finalmente|Angelotti","There is no prelude: the opening chords are Scarpia\u2019s motif, and Angelotti bursts straight in.","The opera begins without any prelude; the opening chords of the Scarpia motif lead immediately to the agitated appearance of Angelotti","Act_1"),
("music","Te Deum|Tre sbirri","As the Te Deum procession fills the church, Scarpia joins the prayer: \u201cTosca, you make me forget even God!\u201d","Tosca, you make me forget even God!","Act_1"),
("artist","Vissi d.arte","Maria Jeritza, said to be Puccini\u2019s favorite Tosca, once fell while escaping Scarpia and sang \u201cVissi d\u2019arte\u201d lying on the floor. It was such a success that she kept doing it.",["was said to be Puccini's favorite Tosca","Jeritza was the first to deliver \"Vissi d'arte\" from a prone position, having fallen to the stage while eluding the grasp of Scarpia.","Jeritza sang the aria while on the floor thereafter"],"Subsequent_productions"),
("music","Io de. sospiri|^Act III$","For the Roman dawn, Puccini went to Castel Sant\u2019Angelo to hear the matins bells, and had bells cast by four different foundries.",["went to the Castel Sant'Angelo to measure the sound of matins bells there","Puccini had bells for the Roman dawn cast to order by four different foundries."]),
("critic","Io de. sospiri|^Act III$","Librettist Illica grumbled after the premiere that all the fuss and money spent on the bells \u201cpasses completely unnoticed\u201d.","the great fuss and the large amount of money for the bells have constituted an additional folly, because it passes completely unnoticed",None,"Luigi Illica, letter to Ricordi (via Wikipedia)"),
("history","Io de. sospiri","The shepherd boy\u2019s song is in Roman dialect. Puccini asked for a \u201cgood romanesco poet\u201d, and folklorist Giggi Zanazzo wrote the verse.",["The composer asked a friend to have a \"good romanesco poet\" write some words","Luigi \"Giggi\" Zanazzo wrote the verse"],"Adaptation_and_writing"),
("history","O dolci mani","Ricordi noticed that part of \u201cO dolci mani\u201d was borrowed from music Puccini had cut from his early opera Edgar, and demanded changes. There wasn\u2019t time.",["some of the music for Cavaradossi's act 3 aria, \"O dolci mani\" was borrowed from music Puccini had cut from his early opera, Edgar","time was too short before the scheduled January 1900 premiere to make any further changes"],"Adaptation_and_writing"),
("critic",None,"In the 1950s musicologist Joseph Kerman called Tosca a \u201cshabby little shocker\u201d. Conductor Thomas Beecham replied that anything Kerman says about Puccini \u201ccan safely be ignored\u201d.",["described Tosca as a \"shabby little shocker.\"","can safely be ignored"],"Critical_reception"),
("history",None,"The Met premiere was on 4 February 1901, only a year after the world premiere.","The premiere at the Metropolitan Opera was on 4 February 1901"),
("artist","E lucevan","Luciano Pavarotti\u2019s last stage performance was as Cavaradossi at the Met, on 13 March 2004.","Pavarotti's last stage performance was as Cavaradossi at the Met, on 13 March 2004.","Subsequent_productions"),
("history",None,"Sardou\u2019s play La Tosca, written for Sarah Bernhardt, had more than 3,000 performances in France alone.","with more than 3,000 performances in France alone","Background"),
("composer",None,"By cutting several numbers, Puccini left \u201cthree strong characters trapped in an airless, violent, tightly wound melodrama\u201d, as his biographer put it.","leaving three strong characters trapped in an airless, violent, tightly wound melodrama","Adaptation_and_writing","Mary Jane Phillips-Matz, Puccini biography (via Wikipedia)"),
